meta-unihertz
=============

LuneOS machine and kernel support for the Unihertz QWERTY phones, all three of
which are MediaTek MT6771 (Helio P60/P70), arm64, pre-GKI 4.14 ALPS:

    titanpocket   Unihertz Titan Pocket   3.1" 716x720    Android 11 vendor
    titanslim     Unihertz Titan Slim     4.2" 768x1280   Android 11 vendor
    titan         Unihertz Titan          4.6" 1430x1440  Android 10 vendor

Primary target is the **Titan Pocket**.

These machines build a boot image and nothing else. The rootfs comes from
MACHINE=halium-arm64 and the GSI is picked at install time, so the per-device
artefact is one boot.img:

    MACHINE=titanpocket bitbake initramfs-android-image
    MACHINE=titanpocket bitbake linux-unihertz-titanpocket


The kernel, and why it is someone's hobby rather than a vendor drop
------------------------------------------------------------------

Unihertz have never released kernel source for any Titan and have ignored GPL
requests since 2020. Both trees used here are Nikita Ukhrenkov's (TheKit /
NotKit): a MediaTek ALPS BSP with the device-specific drivers disassembled out
of the stock kernel binaries and rewritten - panels, touchscreens, the AW9523
keyboard matrix, camera sensors, the Chipsailing fingerprint reader. Treat him
as the upstream for this port; the Ubuntu Touch adaptation is the reference
implementation of the same hardware on the same libhybris/LXC stack.

    titan                 gitlab.com/ubports/porting/community-ports/android10/
                          unihertz-titan/kernel-alps-mt6771, branch halium-10.0,
                          4.14.141 (the Android-10 ALPS drop)
    titanpocket/titanslim github.com/ubuntu-touch-unihertz-titan/
                          kernel-alps-mt6771, branch halium-11.0,
                          4.14.186 (the Android-11 ALPS drop), built from
                          github.com/shr-distribution/linux, branch
                          unihertz-mt6771/4.14/halium-11.0: that branch with
                          the LuneOS commits on top

**The two trees are at different sublevels in different repositories, so the
Titan cannot share a build with the other two.** The Pocket and the Slim can -
see below.


Status
------

**`MACHINE=titanpocket bitbake linux-unihertz-titanpocket` builds clean** as of
30 Sep 2026 - 1679 tasks, all succeeded - and produces a 17.2 MB header-v2 boot
image that reproduces the stock geometry field for field and carries the stock
dt_table blob byte for byte. This is, as far as anyone has written down, the
first kernel ever built from the halium-11.0 tree.

Getting there took seven patches (now commits on the shr-distribution/linux
branch) and two config changes;
the staging kit's README tabulates them. Five of the seven are real bugs rather
than toolchain noise, including one that only appears because LuneOS enables
CONFIG_VT and one exported function that returns -ENOMEM as a pointer.

The deploy set is ~/webos/LuneOS/staging/titanpocket-staging.

Nothing has run on hardware.

The material risk is specific and worth stating before anyone spends a day on
it: **on the Pocket and the Slim, nobody has ever shown that a kernel built
from this tree boots.** The Ubuntu Touch port for those two devices still packs
the *stock prebuilt* kernel image and builds only loadable modules out of the
source tree; the drivers TheKit recovered (GT1151QM, CHSC5432, the two R63308
panels, is31fl, AW9523) are there, and the defconfigs were regenerated from the
devices' own /proc/config.gz, but the whole-kernel path is untravelled.

The Titan is the opposite: upstream builds that kernel from source and ships
it, and the branch saw commits in September 2026. If the Pocket fights, build
MACHINE=titan first to separate "this family does not work" from "this branch
does not work".

Fallback if the Pocket's source kernel will not come up: pack the *stock*
kernel image the way Ubuntu Touch does, with our initramfs. LuneOS can afford
that better than they can - their entire AppArmor-as-a-loadable-module
contraption exists because Ubuntu Touch confines with AppArmor and we do not.
What we would still need from a stock kernel is namespaces, and that is the
thing to check first: their LXC config disables the PID/IPC/UTS namespaces and
ships a patched liblxc that drops CLONE_NEWIPC, which is the tell.


Can one image serve the Pocket and the Slim?
--------------------------------------------

Probably, and the remaining question is a single pair of symbols.

Already shared, measured rather than assumed:

  - the same repository, branch and revision
  - a BYTE-IDENTICAL dtb: TitanPocket-mt6771.dtb and TitanSlim-mt6771.dtb are
    both md5 1c0ec5e5c084e9bbb559ea99651a2e56, 114,158 bytes, one dt_table
    entry at offset 64, page_size 2048, platform id 0
  - identical boot geometry, header version and page size
  - the same rootfs and the same GSI tarball

The defconfigs differ in eight lines. Seven union cleanly:
CONFIG_ARCH_MTK_PROJECT (inert in this tree - upstream says so in the commit
that merged the Titan's own eMMC/UFS split, and no matching directory exists),
CUSTOM_KERNEL_LCM (a list, by Makefile construction), the two touchscreens, the
image sensors, the cam_cal drivers, the TEE fingerprint symbol, AW9523.

The eighth does not: CONFIG_LCM_WIDTH and CONFIG_LCM_HEIGHT are single strings,
720x720 against 768x1280. recipes-kernel/linux/files/titanpocket-slim-union.cfg
is the ready-to-try union with that conflict written up; settling it needs both
devices in hand.

Note this is *not* available for the Titan even in principle: besides the
different kernel base, its dt_table declares platform id 0 exactly as the
Pocket's does, so a two-entry table would give lk nothing to choose on.


What is deliberately not here yet
---------------------------------

  - **Tier 1 adaptations** (luneos-device-config). The display scaling these
    devices need cannot be derived - q25's 720x720 panel needed its grid unit
    and device pixel ratio tuned by eye, and the Pocket's 716x720 will be worse.
    Guessing values into the tree would be worse than leaving it empty. Take
    them off hardware, the way adaptations/sargo/deviceinfo documents.
  - **udev rules.** The Ubuntu Touch port ships a 265-line rules file for this
    family; most of it duplicates what 65-android.rules already does. Trim
    against that rather than copying.
  - **The keyboard's symbol layers.** This is the one piece of userland work
    that is certain to be needed, and q25 is the worked example: Qt's
    evdevkeyboard plugin reads only EV_KEY and never MSC_SCAN, so Android .kl
    and .kcm files - and the xkb symbol files Ubuntu Touch ships for
    unihertz_vndr/titan and titanpocket - buy nothing. Whatever the AW9523
    driver reports for punctuation is what the shell gets. Audit it the way
    0001-bbqX0kbd-Q20-symbol-layers.patch did, declare any new keycodes in
    input->keybit or the input core drops them silently, and ship the patched
    module through ANDROID_EXTRA_INITRAMFS_IMAGE_INSTALL if the vendor's binary
    would otherwise win.
  - **MTK connectivity.** Generic already: mtk-load-modules.sh in meta-android
    drives the WMT-era modules.load path these devices need, mtk-bt-address.sh
    reads the BT MAC from /mnt/vendor/nvdata/APCFG/APRDEB/BT_Addr, and the
    fstab.$(getprop ro.hardware) selection fix mindphone forced is in place.
  - **A per-machine initramfs machine.conf.** Deliberately not shipped: these
    machines take meta-android's generic one, whose sdcard_partition and
    system_partition are placeholders. That is fine - the initrd finds the
    partitions by name, and only the panic path reads system_partition (radon
    established this, and mindphone shipped copy-pasted numbers that never
    mattered). Add one only if something actually asks for it.
  - **An install kit.** All three unlock and take fastboot, and the Titan has a
    real recovery partition; the Pocket and Slim have dynamic partitions, so
    install the way bluejay does - rootfs.img and android-rootfs.img pushed into
    userdata, nothing written to super. mtkclient over the preloader is the
    recovery path and the only way to read expdb when a kernel dies before USB.


Expect the build to fail first
------------------------------

Neither tree has ever been compiled with anything but the Clang the Android
build selects (r383902). OE builds it with its own GCC, and both MediaTek 4.14
ports that came before hit the same classes of breakage - mindphone needed six
rounds on the MT6739 ALPS tree, athena eleven commits on a 4.19. Expect at
least: obsolete "#alloc" section-flag syntax in vendor asm, -Werror in the MTK
vendor Makefiles, %llx against a 32-bit phys_addr_t, and host-tool C23 fallout
(already handled here with BUILD_CFLAGS -std=gnu17).

Fix those on a branch of shr-distribution/linux rather than as layer patches -
mindphone's 4.14 work lives on dnim/4.14.186 and athena's on key2/4.19 - and
split build plumbing from real bugs the way athena's notes describe. The
Pocket and the Slim build unihertz-mt6771/4.14/halium-11.0, which carries them
as commits; the Titan builds its halium-10.0 tree as it is, with no commits of
ours yet.


Verification gates before hardware
----------------------------------

  mer_verify_kernel_config on the built .config, not on the defconfig - a
  savedefconfig-minimal file says nothing about symbols left at their default
  (~/webos/gsigki/mer-kernel-check).

  Check the produced boot image against the stock one field for field, the way
  radon's check-bootimg.py does. Header v2, page 2048, kernel 0x40080000,
  ramdisk 0x55000000, tags and dtb 0x54000000, and the dtb section still the
  stock dt_table blob.

  Never build a dtb or dtbo for these devices. radon bricked twice on a
  self-built dtbo, which dies inside lk with no log, no console and no
  recovery signal - indistinguishable from a dead phone.
